<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-1-3-conditionals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Conditionals and Biconditionals</title>

  <subsection xml:id="conditionals">
    <title>Conditionals</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Conditionals are statements that are logical inferences:
        statements of the form <q><em>If <m>p</m>, then
        <m>q</m>.</em></q> We write such inferences as
        <m>p \implies q</m> and read it as <q><m>p</m> <em>implies</em>
        <m>q</m>.</q>
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Note:</term> Though this language (<q><m>p</m> implies
        <m>q</m></q>) is very common, it is often misleading. This
        language might make you think that <m>q</m> can be, in some
        sense, <em>deduced</em> from <m>p</m>, and that the truth of
        <m>p</m> is necessary for <m>p \implies q</m> to be meaningful.
        This is not the case, as we will soon see.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are the lengths of the legs of a
          right triangle with hypotenuse <m>c</m>, then
          <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Or:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          If 288 is divisible by 9, then 288 is divisible by 3.
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        The truth of <m>p</m> gives us information to update our
        knowledge about the truth of <m>q</m>; that is, the truth of
        <m>q</m> is <em>conditioned</em> on <m>p</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>Def. Let <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> be propositions. The
          conditional statement <m>p \implies q</m> is the proposition
          <q>if <m>p</m>, then <m>q</m>.</q> The conditional statement
          <m>p \implies q</m> is <term>false</term> when <m>p</m> is
          true and <m>q</m> is false, and <term>true</term> otherwise.
          In the conditional statement <m>p \implies q</m>, <m>p</m> is
          called the <em>hypothesis</em> (or <em>antecedent</em> or
          <em>premise</em>) and <m>q</m> is called the
          <em>conclusion</em> (or <em>consequence</em>).</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        As a truth table:
      </p>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><m>p</m></cell>
          <cell><m>q</m></cell>
          <cell><m>p \implies q</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        The first two rows are liekly expected, based on the discussion
        above.
      </p>

      <p>
        A conditional statement that is true by virtue of the fact that
        its hypothesis is false (i.e., the bottom two rows) is called
        <term>vacuously true</term>, because the statement itself is
        true, it just does not apply in this particular situation. For
        example, consider our example from above:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are the lengths of the legs of a
          right triangle with hypotenuse <m>c</m>, then
          <m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        If <m>(a, b, c)</m> is not a Pythagorean triple (i.e., <m>p</m>
        is false), the <em>implication</em> is still true, it's just not
        relevant.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection xml:id="representation-as-an-or-statement">
      <title>Representation as an <q>Or</q> Statement</title>

      <p>
        I claim: <m>p \implies q \equiv q \lor \lnot p</m>.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          Proof:
        </p>

        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>p</m></cell>
            <cell><m>q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>p \implies q</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\lnot p</m></cell>
            <cell><m>q \lor \lnot p</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
            <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This is more profound than it seems. This implies that logical
        inferences can be reduced to simpler logical connectives. This
        is how computers implement more sophisticated logical
        operations. Computers cannot natively handle implications, but
        they can handle AND, OR, and NOT (using voltage signals and
        transistors).
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="contrapositive-converse-and-inverse">
      <title>Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          There are three different types of statements that are related
          to implications:
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <paragraphs xml:id="contrapositive">
        <title>Contrapositive</title>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Def. The <term>contrapositive</term> of the statement
            <q>If <m>p</m>, then <m>q</m></q> (i.e.,
            <m>p \implies q</m>) is the statement <q>If not <m>q</m>,
            then not <m>p</m></q> (i.e.,
            <m>\lnot q \implies \lnot p</m>).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>Important Fact</term>: Implications are logically
          equivalent to their contrapositives. That is,
          <m>p \implies q \equiv \lnot q \implies \lnot p</m>.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            Proof. Homework.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Why is this so important? Because it can often simplify
          difficult problems into much simpler ones.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            Example: What is the converse of <q>If <m>n^2</m> is even,
            then <m>n</m> is even</q>? It is <q>If <m>n</m> is odd, then
            <m>n^2</m> is odd.</q> This is much easier to prove!
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="converse">
        <title>Converse</title>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Def. The <term>converse</term> of the statement <q>If
            <m>p</m>, then <m>q</m></q> (i.e., <m>p \implies q</m>) is
            the statement <q>If <m>q</m>, then <m>p</m></q> (i.e., $ q
            p$).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          Is a conditional logically equivalent to its converse? No!
          (Prove this.)
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="inverse">
        <title>Inverse</title>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Def. The <term>inverse</term> of the statement <q>If
            <m>p</m>, then <m>q</m></q> (i.e., <m>p \implies q</m>) is
            the statement <q>If not <m>p</m>, then not <m>q</m></q>
            (i.e., <m>\lnot p \implies \lnot q</m>).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
          Is a conditional logically equivalent to its inverse? No!
          (Prove this.)
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="biconditionals">
    <title>Biconditionals</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>Def. Let <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> be propositions. The
          biconditional statement <m>p \iff q</m> is the proposition
          <q><m>p</m> if and only if <m>q</m>.</q> The biconditional
          statement <m>p \iff q</m> is true when <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>
          have the same truth values, and is false otherwise.
          Biconditional statements are also called
          <em>bi-implications</em>.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><m>p</m></cell>
          <cell><m>q</m></cell>
          <cell><m>p \iff q</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>F</m></cell>
          <cell><m>T</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        Claim:
        <m>p \iff q \equiv (p \implies q) \land (q \implies p)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Note: Since <m>p \implies q</m> can be written in terms of AND,
        OR, and NOT, <m>p \iff q</m> can also be written in terms of
        AND, OR, and NOT.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection xml:id="necessary-and-sufficient-conditions">
      <title>Necessary and Sufficient Conditions</title>

      <p>
        If <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are statements:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><m>p</m> is a <term>sufficient condition</term> for
          <m>q</m> means <q>if <m>p</m> then <m>q</m>.</q></li>
          <li><m>p</m> is a <term>necessary condition</term> for
          <m>q</m> means <q>if not <m>p</m> then not <m>q</m>.</q></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Using the contrapositive, we see that <m>p</m> is a
        <term>necessary condition</term> for <m>q</m> equivalently means
        <q>if <m>q</m> then <m>p</m>.</q> Hence, <m>p \iff q</m> means
        that <m>p</m> is a necessary and sufficient condition for
        <m>q</m> (and equivalently <m>q</m> is a necessary and
        sufficient condition for <m>p</m>).
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>
